"**(1) Draft-plan claims nobody stress-tested**\n- OPRD's *own* headline (\"beats OPD via sampling-variance avoidance + LM-head bottleneck\") never got a claim verdict. C1\u2013C10 attack the plan's extensions but assume OPRD's base result holds. If OPRD's 1.5B advantage over a *well-tuned* OPD is itself inside seed noise (reviewers flag single-run, no CIs everywhere), every downstream phase is moot.\n- Theorem 1 \"zero-variance gradient\" is invoked repeatedly (to refute C6/C8) but never itself checked \u2014 it's deterministic *given fixed rollouts*, yet rollouts are on-policy and drift, so gradient variance across steps may not actually be zero. That load-bearing assumption props up multiple verdicts.\n- The eval instrument: AIME (~30 problems), Avg@16, contamination, and seed variance are treated as background, never as a claim. No power analysis exists \u2014 reviewers demand CIs but nobody estimated how many seeds resolve a 1\u20132pt effect. This is the silent killer.\n- \"All-layer, last-2000-token\" default: assumed, never ablated as a claim.\n\n**(2) Angles of attack not searched**\n- Information-theoretic: no data-processing-inequality / sufficiency argument for why hidden-state (pre-head) supervision could carry signal the logits can't \u2014 this is the *formal* version of the LM-head-bottleneck story and would ground or kill it independent of ARH/PRH.\n- Optimization dynamics: no NTK / loss-landscape / gradient-SNR comparison of MSE-regression vs KL objectives (MSE is convex-in-output, KL is not) \u2014 the most likely *real* reason one beats the other, entirely unsearched.\n- Representation-target staleness under synchronous on-policy hidden-state matching: flagged as an unfilled gap in two sweeps, never searched.\n- Teacher-student capacity-gap literature (larger teacher \u2260 better distillation) \u2014 directly relevant to cross-arch E, unsearched.\n\n**(3) Internal contradictions**\n- C6 (posbias transfer, \"medium,\" argues OPRD may *reverse* OPD's bias \u2192 genuinely interesting) conflicts with the A1 judgment calling A1 \"confirmatory/near-definitional, high confirmatory risk.\" One says direction is open; the other says answer is known. Reconcile before scoping A1.\n- The plan treats full-Gram/CKA as a \"weak PRH strawman control,\" but sweep-update-geometry + sweep-relational-kd both cite CKA-QAD *beating* output matching. Judgments acknowledge it, but Phase-B/E control sets are still built around the strawman framing. The control is a live competitor, not a floor.\n\n**(4) Single most important unresolved question**\nDoes *any* hidden-state objective beat a well-tuned OPD baseline at 1.5B with \u22655 seeds and CIs? Until that's answered on the actual eval, relational-vs-pointwise and PRH-vs-ARH are premature \u2014 you'd be optimizing the second decimal of an unverified first digit."